    body: `Most final-year projects that go badly do not go badly because the team lacked the ability. They go badly because the project was the wrong size, and that was decided in the first two weeks.

## Start from a problem you can see

The strongest projects we see start from something the team has personally observed. A canteen that throws away food, a lab register nobody can read, an irrigation timer that runs in the rain.

This matters for a practical reason: if you can see the problem, you can measure it, and if you can measure it you can prove your project did something. A project taken from a list of ideas usually has no accessible baseline, so at the end you cannot say whether it worked.

## Scope to one semester, then add

The reliable test: **could you finish a crude, working version in six weeks?** If not, the project is too big, regardless of how interesting it is.

A working crude version beats an ambitious incomplete one on every axis that matters — you can test it, you can show it, and you can build the ambitious version on top of it if time allows.

- Write down what "done" means before you start
- Cut the feature you are least sure about, immediately
- Plan for the prototype to fail twice, because it will

## Check whether it has been done in your own college

This is the step almost everyone skips. Search your college's project archive before you commit. You will find one of three things:

1. Nobody has done it — proceed.
2. Someone has done it badly — that is a good project, and say so explicitly.
3. Someone has done it well — **build on it**. This is better than starting fresh, not worse.

Building on a previous group's work is not a lesser project. It is how research actually works, and it means you spend your semester on the new part rather than rebuilding the sensing rig that already exists.

## Pick something you will still care about in month four

Enthusiasm carries the first six weeks. After that the project is debugging, writing and testing, and interest is what gets you through it. Choose accordingly.`,
