    keywords: ["group project", "free riding", "team contribution", "student teamwork"],
    body: `Almost every student has been in a group where one person did very little and received the same grade. It is the most universally felt problem in coursework and the one least often addressed directly.

## Why it persists

Three reasons, and none of them is that faculty do not care:

1. **Contribution is invisible.** Work happens in a chat and a shared folder. By submission there is no record of who did what.
2. **Reporting it feels like betrayal.** Students consistently choose an unfair grade over an uncomfortable conversation.
3. **By the time it is obvious, it is too late.** Raised in the final week, there is nothing anyone can do.

## What actually helps

**Make contribution visible early, and to everyone.** Not as surveillance — as feedback. A group that can see, in week three, that one member has closed no tasks will usually correct itself without anyone escalating. The same information delivered in week fourteen is only useful for allocating blame.

**Assign ownership, not tasks.** "Rohit owns the sensor calibration" creates a clear boundary. "We will all work on the hardware" does not, and it is where contribution quietly disappears.

**Have the conversation in week four.** Not about effort or commitment — about the specific thing that did not happen. It is a much easier conversation early, and it works far more often than people expect.

## If it does not resolve

Raise it with your supervisor before the final month, with specifics rather than an impression. A supervisor who is told in week six can restructure the work. A supervisor told in week fifteen can only adjust a grade, which helps nobody learn anything.

Keep a record as you go — not to build a case, but because reconstructing four months of work from memory in an argument is impossible and unpleasant.`,
