    body: `Publishing a project makes it findable, citable and something you can point an employer at. It is also permanent, so it is worth deciding deliberately.

## Publish if any of these is true

- **It produced a measurement nobody had.** Even a modest project that quantified something previously unmeasured is genuinely useful to the next person.
- **It failed in an instructive way.** A documented failure with a clear cause is more useful to a future team than a vague success.
- **Someone could build on it.** If your calibration, dataset or design would save another group a month, publishing it is the whole point.

## Do not publish if

- It contains personal data of identifiable people without their consent.
- It is patentable and not yet filed — use an embargo instead, which lets the work be cited without being disclosed.
- The result is a claim you cannot support. A published overclaim follows you.

## Before you publish

Write the abstract last, and write it for someone outside your department. If you cannot explain what you did in three sentences without jargon, the report probably needs work too.

Check that every number in the results has its conditions attached, and that the limitations section is honest. The point of publishing is that other people rely on it.`,
