    body: `Institutions now report on how student work contributes to the Sustainable Development Goals. The exercise is useful when the mapping is honest and worthless when it is not.

## The failure mode

Claiming six goals for a project that touches one. A water-monitoring project is SDG 6, plausibly 3 and 11. It is not SDG 5, and tagging it so makes every other tag on the platform less trustworthy.

Over-tagging is not harmless. It is the reason SDG reporting has a credibility problem, and a report where everything claims everything conveys no information.

## A workable test

For each goal you are considering, complete this sentence with something specific:

> *This project contributes to [goal] because it [verb] [measurable thing] for [whom].*

If you cannot fill it in with a measurable thing, drop the goal.

## Primary and secondary

Most projects have **one** primary goal and one or two genuine secondary ones. Tag it that way. A project reducing irrigation water use is primarily SDG 6; secondarily 2, because water efficiency affects yield; arguably 13. Three is a defensible claim; six is not.

## Why it matters beyond the report

A student searching for work related to clean water should find projects that are genuinely about clean water. Every tenuous tag makes that search worse for the next person.`,
